    ("Apricot", "Prunus armeniaca", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "warm", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung, Large Intestine", ["Dryness", "Dry cough", "Constipation", "Mild deficiency"], "Apricot fruit is sweet-sour and gently warming, used as a moistening orchard fruit in food-therapy comparisons.", "Kernels are a different medicinal item and can be toxic if misused. Use caution with fruit allergy or blood sugar management.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Apricots can fruit in warm sheltered UK sites, especially fan-trained against a sunny wall, but blossom is frost-sensitive.", "A UK-edge orchard fruit for mild lung and bowel moistening comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Apricots.jpg"),
    ("Peach", "Prunus persica", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "warm", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung, Large Intestine, Stomach", ["Dryness", "Constipation", "Thirst", "Mild deficiency"], "Peach fruit is sweet-sour and warming, often compared with moistening and fluid-supporting food qualities.", "Peach kernels are medicinal and not the same as the fruit. Use caution with allergy, reflux, or blood sugar management.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Peaches can grow in warm sheltered UK positions, often best fan-trained with frost protection at blossom.", "A warm soft fruit for gentle moistening food-therapy notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Peaches.jpg"),
    ("Nectarine", "Prunus persica var. nucipersica", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "warm", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung, Stomach", ["Dryness", "Thirst", "Mild deficiency", "Digestive support"], "Nectarine is treated much like peach as a sweet-sour warming fruit that supports fluids and mild nourishment.", "Use caution with stone-fruit allergy, reflux, or blood sugar management.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Nectarines need warm sheltered UK sites and blossom protection; greenhouse or fan training improves reliability.", "A peach-like fruit for warm sweet-sour dietary comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nectarines.jpg"),
    ("Grape", "Vitis vinifera", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "neutral", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung, Spleen, Kidney", ["Fatigue", "Dryness", "Thirst", "Mild deficiency"], "Grapes are sweet-sour and neutral, used in food-therapy language for fluids, mild qi-blood support, and dryness.", "Use caution with blood sugar management, reflux, or grape allergy; dried grapes are more concentrated.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Outdoor grapes can crop in sunny sheltered UK sites; greenhouse vines are more reliable.", "A UK-growable vine fruit for sweet-sour fluid and mild deficiency support.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Table%20grapes.jpg"),
    ("Fig", "Ficus carica", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "neutral", "Sweet", "Lung, Spleen, Large Intestine", ["Constipation", "Dryness", "Dry cough", "Mild deficiency"], "Fig is sweet and moistening, often used as food support for dry bowel and lung dryness patterns.", "Use caution with diarrhea, latex allergy, blood sugar management, or high-fibre sensitivity.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Figs grow well in warm sheltered UK sites, especially against walls or in containers with restricted roots.", "A practical UK-edge fruit for moistening dryness and bowel support.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Figs.jpg"),
    ("Quince", "Cydonia oblonga", "fruit", "Astringe and stabilize", "warm", "Sour, Sweet", "Spleen, Stomach, Liver", ["Digestive support", "Loose stool", "Food stagnation", "Dampness"], "Quince is sour-sweet and gently warming, fitting astringent digestive and food-stagnation comparisons when cooked.", "Usually cooked rather than eaten raw. Use caution with constipation, reflux, or high sugar preserves.", "hardy_outdoors", "Quince trees are hardy in much of the UK and crop in sunny sheltered positions.", "A cooked orchard fruit for sour astringent digestion-support notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Quince%20fruit.jpg"),
    ("Medlar", "Mespilus germanica", "fruit", "Astringe and stabilize", "neutral", "Sweet, Astringent", "Spleen, Stomach, Large Intestine", ["Loose stool", "Digestive support", "Mild deficiency", "Food stagnation"], "Medlar is a sweet-astringent fruit best compared with gentle stabilization and digestion support after bletting.", "Eat only properly bletted ripe fruit. Use caution with constipation or high sugar preparations.", "hardy_outdoors", "Medlar is hardy and grows well in UK orchards and gardens.", "A traditional UK orchard fruit for sweet-astringent food therapy comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mespilus%20germanica%20fruit.jpg"),
    ("Damson", "Prunus domestica subsp. insititia", "fruit", "Astringe and stabilize", "cool", "Sour, Sweet", "Liver, Large Intestine, Stomach", ["Loose stool", "Thirst", "Digestive support", "Dryness"], "Damson is sour and cooling, useful as a local fruit comparison for astringency, fluids, and mild digestive support.", "Usually cooked with sugar. Use caution with reflux, diarrhea, or blood sugar management.", "hardy_outdoors", "Damsons are hardy and reliable UK fruit trees or hedgerow trees.", "A sour local orchard fruit for cooling astringent comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Damsons.jpg"),
    ("Blueberry", "Vaccinium corymbosum", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "cool", "Sweet, Sour", "Liver, Kidney, Stomach", ["Dry eyes", "Dryness", "Heat signs", "Mild deficiency"], "Blueberry is best mapped as a cooling sweet-sour fruit with liver-kidney and fluid-support associations.", "Use caution with berry allergy, blood sugar management, or anticoagulants in concentrated extracts.", "hardy_outdoors", "Blueberries grow outdoors in the UK in acidic soil or ericaceous containers.", "A UK garden soft fruit for cooling sweet-sour deficiency and eye-support notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blueberries.jpg"),
    ("Cranberry", "Vaccinium macrocarpon", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cool", "Sour", "Bladder, Kidney", ["Urinary discomfort", "Damp-heat", "Thirst", "Heat signs"], "Cranberry is sour and cooling, fitting urinary damp-heat and fluid-support food-therapy comparisons.", "Use caution with anticoagulants, kidney stones, reflux, or high-sugar preparations.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Cranberries can be grown in acidic wet containers or bog-style beds in the UK, though they need care.", "A sour cooling fruit for urinary-pattern dietary notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cranberries.jpg"),
    ("Elderberry", "Sambucus nigra Fructus", "fruit", "Release the exterior", "cool", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung", ["Common cold", "Fever", "Dryness", "Mild deficiency"], "Cooked elderberry is sour-sweet and cooling, commonly used as seasonal food support in cold-season contexts.", "Raw elder parts can be toxic; cook ripe berries properly. Use caution with autoimmune disease, pregnancy, or digestive sensitivity.", "hardy_outdoors", "Elder is common and hardy in UK hedgerows and gardens.", "A local cooked fruit for seasonal Lung-oriented food therapy.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sambucus%20nigra%20fruits.jpg"),
    ("Mulberry", "Morus nigra Fructus", "fruit", "Tonify deficiency", "cold", "Sweet, Sour", "Liver, Kidney", ["Dryness", "Dizziness", "Constipation", "Blood deficiency"], "Mulberry fruit is traditionally sweet-sour and cooling, associated with nourishing yin-blood and moistening dryness.", "Use caution with diarrhea, cold digestion, blood sugar management, or berry allergy.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Mulberries can grow in sheltered UK gardens, though trees are slow and crop best in warm sites.", "A cooling dark fruit for yin-blood and dryness comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mulberries.jpg"),
    ("Redcurrant", "Ribes rubrum", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cool", "Sour, Sweet", "Lung, Stomach", ["Thirst", "Heat signs", "Digestive support", "Dryness"], "Redcurrant is sour-sweet and cooling, useful for thirst and light heat-clearing food comparisons.", "Use caution with reflux, diarrhea, berry allergy, or blood sugar management in preserves.", "hardy_outdoors", "Redcurrants are hardy and productive in UK gardens.", "A reliable UK soft fruit for sour cooling food-therapy notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Redcurrants.jpg"),
    ("Whitecurrant", "Ribes rubrum White Group", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cool", "Sweet, Sour", "Lung, Stomach", ["Thirst", "Dryness", "Heat signs", "Digestive support"], "Whitecurrant is a milder sweet-sour cooling fruit, fitting fluid generation and summer heat comparisons.", "Use caution with reflux, diarrhea, berry allergy, or sweet preserves.", "hardy_outdoors", "Whitecurrants grow well outdoors in the UK and tolerate cool climates.", "A gentle UK soft fruit for cooling and fluid-support notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/White%20currants.jpg"),
    ("Kiwi fruit", "Actinidia deliciosa", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cold", "Sweet, Sour", "Stomach, Bladder", ["Thirst", "Constipation", "Heat signs", "Digestive support"], "Kiwi is cold, sweet-sour, and fluid-rich, suitable for heat, thirst, and dry bowel comparisons.", "Use caution with kiwi allergy, reflux, cold digestion, diarrhea, or latex-fruit syndrome.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Hardy kiwi and some kiwi cultivars can grow in sheltered UK sites; fruiting needs warmth and suitable pollination.", "A cool sour fruit for bowel-moistening and heat-clearing food notes.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kiwi%20fruit.jpg"),
    ("Kiwi berry", "Actinidia arguta", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cool", "Sweet, Sour", "Stomach, Bladder", ["Thirst", "Constipation", "Heat signs", "Dryness"], "Kiwi berry is a cool sweet-sour fruit, similar in direction to kiwi but often more practical for UK gardens.", "Use caution with kiwi allergy, reflux, or cold digestion.", "sheltered_outdoors", "Hardy kiwi berry vines can grow in sheltered UK gardens and need support plus pollination planning.", "A UK-plausible vine fruit for cooling fluid-support comparisons.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Actinidia%20arguta%20fruit.jpg"),
    ("Melon", "Cucumis melo", "fruit", "Clear heat", "cold", "Sweet", "Heart, Stomach, Bladder", ["Summerheat", "Thirst", "Urinary discomfort", "Heat signs"], "Melon is cold and sweet, classically aligned with summer heat, thirst, and urination support as food.", "Use caution with cold digestion, diarrhea, or blood sugar management.", "greenhouse", "Melons need warmth and are best grown in a greenhouse or very sheltered sunny UK spot.", "A cooling greenhouse fruit for summerheat food therapy.", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cantaloupe%20and%20cross%20section.jpg"),
